> What's the command to un-apply the old patch so I can apply this one?

Try:

patch -Rp1 <old_patch

That should reverse the effects of the old patch and then you can apply 
the new. Btw. this information can be deducted from the output that you 
get if you try to apply the new patch without reversing the earlier or 
by just typing 'patch --help' at the command prompt.

If that fails here are other ways to do it:

Copy read.c from the official distribution over the one in your patched 
directory. (Never patch the original copy of official source 
distribution. Make first a copy of the sources and patch that.)

Another way to do is manually insert those five lines in the end of the 
patch into the proper place in your exsisting patched read.c.

Yet another is just try patching your sources. If you save this patch as 
plonk.diff in the root of your nethack source tree and give the 
following command in that root directory:

patch -lp1 <plonk.diff

You might need to answer some queries that patch asks but then patch 
should only complain about the first three chunks but apply the fourth.

